WEBVTT

0
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.760
Welcome back.

1
00:00:05.179 --> 00:00:06.400
On a single server,

2
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:07.940
time is simple.

3
00:00:09.189 --> 00:00:10.420
Event A happens,

4
00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.029
then event B happens.

5
00:00:13.689 --> 00:00:19.079
But when you spread a single AI workload across a massive distributed cluster,

6
00:00:19.329 --> 00:00:23.950
assuming distributed systems share a perfectly consistent view of time,

7
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.489
can quickly become problematic.

8
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.450
In distributed AI

9
00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:31.479
event order is everything.

10
00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.909
We aren't just moving independent files,

11
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:41.430
we are actively updating the interdependent weights of a massive neural network.

12
00:00:41.669 --> 00:00:43.939
If those updates happen out of sequence,

13
00:00:44.229 --> 00:00:46.290
the model doesn't just slow down.

14
00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:50.310
The training process can become unstable.

15
00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:56.240
Imagine you have 2 GPUs computing different parts of the same problem.

16
00:00:57.639 --> 00:01:00.709
GPU 1 finishes its calculation first,

17
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.810
followed by GPU 2,

18
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.629
but because of a temporary network delay,

19
00:01:05.959 --> 00:01:09.419
GPU 2's data arrives at the destination

20
00:01:09.879 --> 00:01:11.360
before GPU 1.

21
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:17.480
If the destination note simply accepts the data in the order it physically arrived,

22
00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:20.830
the expected sequence of updates is disrupted.

23
00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:25.569
The model applies the 2nd step before the 1st step.

24
00:01:27.879 --> 00:01:31.099
The mathematical state of the entire neural network

25
00:01:31.099 --> 00:01:33.980
may diverge from the expected training state.

26
00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.540
This is why distributed AI systems place such

27
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:43.080
heavy emphasis on coordination and consistency across nodes.

28
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.900
They cannot depend entirely on physical arrival order

29
00:01:48.199 --> 00:01:49.660
because network behavior

30
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.400
is inherently variable.

31
00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.160
Distributed systems don't just move data,

32
00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.110
they coordinate behavior.

33
00:01:58.239 --> 00:02:03.199
Every single node in the cluster must maintain a sufficiently consistent view

34
00:02:03.199 --> 00:02:08.429
of update ordering of events before any model weights are actually updated.

35
00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:10.699
The system forces nodes to wait,

36
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.679
verify the sequence,

37
00:02:12.919 --> 00:02:16.440
and apply updates in a tightly coordinated sequence.

38
00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:23.690
We intentionally trade raw speed for system coordination and consistency.

39
00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:25.139
Because

40
00:02:25.449 --> 00:02:26.240
in AI

41
00:02:26.449 --> 00:02:27.830
doing things fast

42
00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:31.880
is far less valuable if you do them in the wrong order.

43
00:02:32.669 --> 00:02:33.940
In the next lesson,

44
00:02:34.229 --> 00:02:38.080
we'll look at the specific infrastructure cost of that trade-off

45
00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.470
as we compare network latency

46
00:02:40.669 --> 00:02:42.550
to synchronization overhead.